ist James Clerk Maxwell discovered the laws that\nL422@P3: Sikka, Chief Executive Officer and Managing Direc\u0002tor of Infosys. “This is the beginning of a human rev\u0002olution, and institutions like IAS have long served to\nL484@P4: serious kinds of illicit or illegal sex, such as the child of a nun or priest, or the child of\nL485@P4: adultery or incest. The relationship of the bachelor Duke Robert and his concubine\nL486@P4: Herleva, if not a formal marriage, did not offend on these grounds. Nor, evidently, did\nL487@P4: anyone at the time question Herleva’s sexual honor, or, as a result, William’s paternity.\nL488@P4: What, then, about William’s parentage did offend? Why was he called bastard?\nL500@P4: Bede, and Hrabanus Maurus, all define nothus as a child of mixed\nL501@P4: parentage, of a noble father and an ignoble mother.\nL502@P4: This preoccupation with mixed social status, this distaste for\nL503@P4: rulers with lower-status mothers, persists in subsequent centuries.\nL504@P4: Chroniclers of the twelfth and thirteenth centuries explicitly\nL505@P4: describe as nothus or spurius, as illegitimate sons born to lower-status\nL506@P4: mothers, not only William of Normandy (d. 1087), but also the\nL517@P4: it is said, developed the idea that a bastard was a child born in the saddle, in French,\nL518@P4: fils de bast. We can understand this as indicating birth outside of a marriage bed, in\nL519@P4: transit. However, we also find the term used, like nothus, to indicate a union of per\u0002sons born to parents of mixed social status. As one late-eleventh-century chronicler\nL520@P4: declared, the French called William bastard because of his mixed parentage: He bore\nL591@P5: and its scale dependence), we can describe all of its\nL592@P5: basic properties. Yet, the universe is very strange:\nL593@P5: atoms make up only 5 percent of the density of the\nL594@P5: universe. Dark matter, most likely composed of a yet\nL595@P5: undiscovered new particle, comprises most of the\nL596@P5: mass in our galaxy. Most of the energy in the\nL597@P5: universe is in the form of dark energy, energy\nL598@P5: associated with empty space.\nL599@P5: How do you free your thinking? I need to first get\nL600@P5: away from distractions and set aside a block of time\nL711@P6: while in residence at the Institute for Advanced Study concerning the relationship\nL712@P6: between Islamic law and international law. The issue of international child abduc\u0002tion offers a useful case study to put the stakes of this question into stark relief.\nL713@P6: International child abduction is a particular phenomenon that implicitly reflects\nL714@P6: the complex implications of a globalized economic environment. Generally, this\nL715@P6: form of ab duc tion occurs in the context of mar\u0002ital breakdown, where one parent has dual\nL736@P6: hardly unusual for legal systems generally. Consider the United States federal sys\u0002tem with its fifty states. Each state might have a different approach to a particular\nL737@P6: legal issue. And if there were no U.S. Supreme Court to resolve the difference,\nL738@P6: then those fifty different opinions would remain authoritative law within their\nL739@P6: respective jurisdictions. That sort of plurality historically has characterized Islamic\nL740@P6: law, though the conditions that delimited jurisdiction have been of a different sort.\nL746@P6: states, family law has taken the shape of a statute generally called personal status\nL747@P6: laws (Arabic: al-ahwal al-shakhsiyya). As a statute, it is codified in a uniform fash\u0002ion; many have suggested that this new legislative form marks a fundamental\nL748@P6: departure from the early plurality that characterized Islamic law. Without taking\nL749@P6: sides on this issue, we can at the very least note that these statutes bear the imprint\nL750@P6: of an Islamic legal past. They frame issues of marriage, divorce, inheritance, and\nL751@P6: child custody in the distinct term and language of the premodern Islamic legal tra\u0002dition. These statutes, therefore, represent the site where Islamic law is introduced\nL752@P6: into modern legal systems through the operation of the state and its legislative\nL753@P6: powers. Islamic law today does not exist in the air, so to speak. It is mediated\nL754@P6: through the state, taking specific shape through the complex interaction of differ\u0002ent (and at times oppositional) state institutions.\nL755@P6: All this insight into Islamic law, however, misses one important part of our story,\nL756@P6: namely that Maria has a custody order from a U.S. court. Doesn’t that matter in\nL757@P6: this case? That will depend on whether or not Pakistan will recognize a foreign cus\u0002tody order. To address this question requires a discussion of private international\nL758@P6: law. Private international law, also called conflicts of law in the common law\nL759@P6: world, is a regime of law that hovers in the background in any and all legal disputes.\nL760@P6: It only comes to the aid of a judge when a case involves a foreign legal element.\nL761@P6: Foreign legal elements can arise in a wide range of cases and force a judge in one\nL762@P6: country to determine whether and to what extent the law of a different country is\nL763@P6: applicable. In other words, private international law asks the judge to consider and\nL764@P6: possibly apply the law of an “other,” or rather his own legal system’s “legal other.”\nL765@P6: In this case, it arises the minute Maria presents the U.S. court’s custody order to a\nL766@P6: Pakistani court. Will the Pakistani court yield to the determination of a U.S. court\nL767@P6: on the issue of child custody? To do so might\nL768@P6: appear as if the Pakistani judge is deferring to\nL769@P6: the U.S. court, relinquishing his judicial role, or\nL770@P6: subverting the sovereign interest of Pakistan’s\nL771@P6: legal order to that of the United States. As it\nL772@P6: turns out, Pakistani case law will not support\nL773@P6: Maria’s claim for her children. The foreign custody\nL774@P6: order may have no impact at all whatsoever.\nL775@P6: Rather, the foreign custody order puts into stark\nL776@P6: relief the ongoing significance of state sover\u0002eignty in the formation and imperatives of law.\nL777@P6: In 1980, the Hague Conference issued a\nL778@P6: convention concerning these forms of interna\u0002tional child abduction. The Hague Abduction\nL779@P6: Convention required signatories to create cen\u0002tral authorities that would handle any claim\nL780@P6: concerning international child abduction.\nL781@P6: These central authorities are important sites for\nL782@P6: distributing information, training domestic\nL857@P7: are beautifully anachronistic. Yet the genius in Wiley’s work is that it leads us to\nL858@P7: question: Why can’t urban African Americans assume the delicate harmony and\nL859@P7: militant posture reminiscent of a Renaissance master? This book seeks to similarly\nL860@P7: restage urban blacks within societal institu tions and fields of power from which\nL861@P7: they are often presumed to be excluded. Despite the statistical odds against their\nL862@P7: dreams coming to fruition in such a context, I foreground the resilience it takes\nL863@P7: for black Chicagoans to keep dreaming anyway. __________________________\nL864@P7: There are so many kinds of dreams. But in the long tradition of African American\nL865@P7: activism, dreams have typically been linked to concrete aspirations for social\nL866@P7: reform. In his 1951 poem “Harlem,” Langston Hughes, writer and social activist,\nL867@P7: famously questioned the outcome of a “dream deferred.” Does such a dream dry\nL868@P7: up, fester, stink, crust and sugar over, or sag like a heavy load, he pondered. Then,\nL951@P7-8: IMAGE COURTESY OF © KEHINDE WILEY STUDIOS, BROOKLYNCédric Villani, Member in the School of Mathematics in\nL952@P8: the spring of 2009 and currently Professor at Université\nL953@P8: Lyon I and Director of the Institut Henri Poincaré, has\nL954@P8: called his stay at the Institute one of his most productive\nL955@P8: periods, during which more than 250 pages were written.\nL956@P8: In his Member report to then-Director Peter Goddard at\nL957@P8: the end of his stay, Villani wrote of his collaboration with\nL958@P8: Clément Mouhot from Paris, “Writing up the paper on\nL959@P8: Landau damping was one of the most intense experiences\nL960@P8: of my professional life: for three months in a row, we\nL961@P8: kept unlocking seemingly untractable obstacles on a\nL962@P8: weekly basis. Our 180-page-long paper solves a fifty\u0002year-old open problem.” A year after his IAS visit,\nL963@P8:  Villani was awarded the 2010 Fields Medal, in part for\nL964@P8: the work that he did at the Institute on his proof of non\u0002linear Landau damping. Following are excerpts from\nL965@P8: Birth of a Theorem, translated by Malcolm DeBevoise\nL966@P8: (Farrar, Straus, and Giroux, 2015), originally published\nL967@P8: in 2012 as Théorème Vivant (Éditions Grasset &\nL968@P8: Fasquelle), which describe his fervent, halting, and very\nL969@P8: human experience in trying to obtain the proof.\nL970@P8: Princeton, January 1, 2009\nL971@P8: Finally, the Institute for Advanced Study—the\nL972@P8: IAS, as everyone calls it—comes into view. A lit\u0002tle like a castle rising up in the middle of a forest. We\nL973@P8: had to go around a large golf course in order to find\nL974@P8: it. . . .\nL975@P8: It is here that Einstein spent the last twenty years\nL976@P8: of his life. True, by the time he came to America he\nL977@P8: was no longer the dashing young man who had revo\u0002lutionized physics in 1905. Nevertheless, his influ\u0002ence on this place was deep and long-lasting, more so\nL978@P8: even than that of John von Neumann, Kurt Gödel,\nL979@P8: Hermann Weyl, Robert Oppenheimer, Ernst Kan\u0002torowicz, or John Nash—great thinkers all, whose\nL980@P8: very names send a shiver down the spine.\nL981@P8: Their successors include Jean Bourgain, Enrico\nL982@P8: Bom bieri, Freeman Dyson, Edward Witten, Vladimir\nL983@P8: Voevod sky, and many others. The IAS, more than\nL984@P8: Harvard, Berkeley, NYU, or any other institution of\nL985@P8: higher learning, can justly claim to be the earthly\nL986@P8: temple of mathematics and theoretical physics. Paris,\nL987@P8: the world capital of mathematics, has many more\nL988@P8: mathematicians. But at the IAS one finds the distil\u0002late, the crème de la crème. Permanent membership\nL989@P8: in the IAS is perhaps the most prestigious academic\nL990@P8: post in the world!\nL1076@P8: far away!! I never thought it would be so hard. No way\nL1077@P8: I could have foreseen the obstacles that lay ahead!\nL1078@P8: The velocities are the problem, the velocities! When\nL1079@P8: there isn’t any dependence with respect to velocity, you\nL1080@P8: can separate the variables by means of a Fourier trans\u0002form, but when you’ve got velocities, what can you do? In\nL1081@P8: a nonlinear equation, velocities are obligatory—there’s no\nL1082@P8: way I can avoid dealing with them!\nL1083@P8: “Are you all right? Really, there’s no point worry\u0002ing yourself sick! Relax, take it easy.”\nL1084@P8: “Can’t.”\nL1085@P8: “You really seem obsessed.”\nL1086@P8: “Look, I’m on a mission. It’s called nonlinear Lan\u0002dau damping.”\nL1087@P8: “I thought you were supposed to be working on the\nL1088@P8: Boltzmann equation. That was your big project,\nL1089@P8: wasn’t it? You don’t want to lose sight of what you\nL1090@P8: came here to do, do you?”\nL1091@P8: “Can’t be helped. Right now it’s Landau damp\u0002ing.”\nL1092@P8: But Landau damping goes on playing the cold,\nL1093@P8: unattainable beauty. I can’t get next to her.\nL1094@P8: . . . Still, there’s that little calculation I did on getting\nL1095@P8: home from the museum—doesn’t that give some reason\nL1096@P8: for hope? But man, is it ever complicated! I added two\nL1097@P8: more parameters to the norm. Our norms used to have\nL1098@P8: five regularity indices, which already was the world\nL1099@P8: record—now they’re going to have seven! But so what,\nL1100@P8: applying the two indices to a function that doesn’t depend\nL1101@P8: on velocity leaves you with the same norm as before,\nL1102@P8: there’s no inconsistency. . . . I’ve got to check the calcula\u0002tion carefully. But if I try to do it right now, it’s going to\nL1103@P8: turn out wrong, so let’s wait until tomorrow! I’m going to\nL1104@P8: have to do the whole damn thing over again, this time with\nL1105@P8: norms that have got seven indices. Good Lord.\nL1106@P8: Seeing how glum I looked, Claire felt sorry for me.\nL1107@P8: Or at least felt she had to do something to cheer me\nL1108@P8: up.\nL1109@P8: Birth of a Theorem\nL1110@P8: Six months at IAS, two-hundred-fifty pages, and a Fields Medal\nL1111@P8: 8\nL1112@P8: Cédric Villani in the Institute woods in 2009\nL1113@P8: (Continued on page 9)\nL1114@P8-9: CLAIRE CALMET9\nL1115@P9: “Look, tomorrow’s Sunday. You can spend the day\nL1116@P9: at the office if you like; I’ll take care of the little lam\u0002bkins.”\nL1117@P9: At that moment, nothing in the world could have\nL1118@P9: pleased me more.\nL1119@P9: Princeton, January 21, 2009\nL1120@P9: Thanks to the rabbit I pulled out of my hat on my\nL1121@P9: returning from the museum the other evening, I’ve\nL1122@P9: been able to get back on track. But today I’m filled\nL1123@P9: with a strange mixture of optimism and dread. Got\nL1124@P9: around one major roadblock: made a few explicit\nL1125@P9: calculations and eventually figured out how to man\u0002age a term that had gotten too big—that much gives\nL1126@P9: me hope. At the same time, the complexity of the\nL1127@P9: mathematical landscape that’s now opened up\nL1128@P9: makes my head spin if I think about it for more than\nL1129@P9: a few moments.\nL1130@P9: Could it really be that Vlasov’s splendid equation,\nL1131@P9: which I thought I was beginning to get a handle on,\nL1132@P9: operates only by fits and starts? On paper, at least, it\nL1133@P9: looks as though sometimes the response to external\nL1134@P9: perturbations suddenly occurs very, very quickly. I’ve\nL1135@P9: never heard of such a thing; it’s not in any of the\nL1136@P9: articles and books that I’ve read. But in any case\nL1137@P9: we’re making progress. __________________________\nL1138@P9: Princeton, February 27, 2009\nL1139@P9: A bit of a party atmosphere at the Institute today\nL1140@P9: now that the five-day workshop on geometric partial\nL1141@P9: differential equations is coming to an end. Very fine\nL1142@P9: casting, with many stars—all the invited speakers\nL1143@P9: agreed to participate.\nL1144@P9: In the seminar room I found a place to stand all\nL1145@P9: the way at the back, behind a large table. Sometimes\nL1146@P9: an audiovisual control board is set up on it, but not\nL1147@P9: today, so I could spread out my notes on top. There’s\nL1148@P9: no better place to be. I was lucky to get there before\nL1149@P9: Peter Sarnak, a permanent professor at the Institute\nL1150@P9: who likes it as much as I do. You can always be sure\nL1151@P9: of staying awake, for one thing. If you’re sitting in a\nL1249@P9: last few weeks—so much, in fact, it was almost com\u0002ical. At night the fireflies transform the oaks and red\nL1250@P9: maples into Christmas trees, romantic, impossibly\nL1251@P9: tall, decorated with innumerable blinking lights.\nL1252@P9: Enormous mushrooms, a small furtive rabbit, the\nL1253@P9: fleeting silhouette of a fox in the night, the startling\nL1254@P9: noises of stray rutting deer . . .\nL1255@P9: In the meantime a lot has happened on the Landau\nL1256@P9: damping front! We were finally able to get the proof to\nL1289@P9: that damping occurs on a gigantic time scale, but not\nL1290@P9: an infinitely long one. And since we were stymied on\nL1291@P9: this point, we were also stymied on regularity—we\nL1292@P9: couldn’t find a way to get out from the analytic frame\u0002work. Very often at the end of my talks somebody\nL1293@P9: would ask one of these two questions: In the case of\nL1294@P9: Coulomb or Newton interaction, does one also have damp\u0002ing in infinite time? Can one do without the analyticity\nL1295@P9: assumption? In either case my response was the same,\nL1296@P9: that I couldn’t say anything without consulting my\nL1297@P9: lawyer first. Honestly, I don’t know whether there’s a\nL1298@P9: profound mystery here, or whether we simply weren’t\nL1299@P9: clever enough to work out the answer. [. . .] ■\nL1300@P9: Recommended Event: Cédric Villani will\nL1301@P9: give a public lecture at the Institute on\nL1302@P9: Tuesday, April 14. For more information,\nL1303@P9: visit www.ias.edu/villani-2015.\nL1304@P9-10: BIRTH OF A THEOREM (Continued from page 8)10\nL1305@P10: BY SUZANNAH CLARK\nL1306@P10: On the second Sunday after Trinity in 1724, the congregation at the\nL1307@P10: Thomaskirche in Leipzig heard Johann Sebastian Bach’s new cantata that\nL1308@P10: began with the words Ach Gott. Bach set the word Gott to the most dissonant triad\nL1309@P10: known at the time: the augmented triad. Bach’s own son, Carl Philipp Emanuel\nL1310@P10: Bach, wrote in the second volume of his treatise of 1762 that the offending aug\u0002mented fifth of this harmony requires careful preparation. His father did not prepare\nL1311@P10: it at all. Acclimatized as we are today to all kinds of\nL1312@P10: dissonances, this harmony might pass the modern lis\u0002tener by. But it would have disconcerted the ears of\nL1313@P10: the eighteenth-century congregation, giving them a\nL1314@P10: God-fearing shudder, while setting the scene for the\nL1315@P10: biblical message of the day. Bach, after all, was setting\nL1316@P10: the tune and words, Ach Gott, vom Himmel sieh darein,\nL1317@P10: that Martin Luther had penned exactly two hundred\nL1318@P10: years earlier, in 1524. Based on Psalm 12, Luther tells\nL1319@P10: of a perilous world filled with those who shun God.\nL1320@P10: The augmented triad has long been a headache for\nL1321@P10: music theorists, only partially on the basis of its harsh\nL1322@P10: sound. Mostly they are perturbed by its construction\nL1323@P10: and their inability to pinpoint a convincing origin for\nL1324@P10: it. It would be no exaggeration to say that, just two\nL1325@P10: years before Bach composed his cantata, the har\u0002monic theory of Jean-Philippe Rameau, a towering\nL1326@P10: figure in the history of music theory, brought about a paradigm shift in how chords\nL1383@P10: B C. Its relative minor pair is A minor, which in its natural minor incarnation con\u0002tains the same pitches but starting from a different point: A B C D E F G A. These\nL1384@P10: two therefore share the same key signature of no sharps or flats. By contrast, the\nL1385@P10: pitches of A harmonic minor are: A B C D E F G# A, which would have necessi\u0002tated the signature of one sharp, G#. This signature is certainly theoretically possi\u0002ble—and was mooted in the nineteenth century—but it never caught on.\nL1929@P14: A decade ago, the geochemical climate record showed hints of a 32-million-year\nL1930@P14: periodicity, with peak cooling taking place a few million years ago, as expected from the\nL1931@P14: last plane passage. Together with Veizer and a third colleague, Andreas Prokoph, we\nL1932@P14: then submitted a first version for publication. However, we actually ended up putting\nL1933@P14: the paper aside for almost a decade because of two nagging inconsistencies.\nL1934@P14: First, analysis of the best database of the kinematics of nearby stars, that of the\nL1946@P14: began to systematically study cosmic ray diffusion in the Milky\nL1947@P14: Way while alleviating the standard assumption that everyone had\nL1948@P14: until then assumed—that the sources are distributed symmetri\u0002cally around the galaxy. To our surprise, it did much more than\nL1949@P14: just explain the meteoritic measurements of a variable cosmic ray\nL1950@P14: flux. It provided an explanation to the so-called Pamela anomaly,\nL1951@P14: a cosmic ray positron excess that was argued by many to be the\nL2122@P15: Save the Date: America’s First National\nL2123@P15: Math Festival Comes to D.C. on April 18\nL2124@P15: New Leadership Appointments\nL2125@P15: Murph Goldberger Rafe Mazzeo\nL2126@P15: Murph Goldberger (1922–2014)\nL2127@P15: INSTITUTE FOR ADVANCED STUDY\nL2128@P15: INFOSYS\nL2129@P15-16: 15Have you moved?\nL2130@P16: Please notify us of your change of address.\nL2131@P16: Send changes to:\nL2132@P16: Public Affairs, Institute for Advanced Study\nL2133@P16: Einstein Drive, Princeton, NJ 08540\nL2134@P16: or email mailings@ias.edu\nL2135@P16: HASSAN\nL2136@P16: ANSARI\nL2137@P16: The Necessity of a Historical\nL2138@P16: Approach to Islamic Theology\nL2139@P16: CÉDRIC\nL2140@P16: VILLANI\nL2141@P16: The Birth of a Theorem\nL2142@P16: NIR\nL2143@P16: SHAVIV\nL2144@P16: A Field Trip Through\nL2145@P16: the Milky Way\nL2146@P16: IASThe InstituteLetter Spring 2015\nL2147@P16: LAURENCE RALPH\nL2148@P16: Renegade Dreams: Living Through\nL2149@P16: Injury in Gangland Chicago\nL2150@P16: The Institute Letter\nL2151@P16: Spring 2015\nL2152@P16: BRUCE M. WHITE\nL2153@P16: Non-Profit Org.\nL2154@P16: U.S. Postage PAID\nL2155@P16: Permit #49\nL2156@P16: Princeton, NJ\nL2157@P16: Robbert Dijkgraaf, Director\nL2158@P16: and Leon Levy Professor\nL2159@P16: Faculty\nL2160@P16: Danielle Allen\nL2161@P16: Nima Arkani-Hamed\nL2162@P16: Yve-Alain Bois\nL2163@P16: Jean Bourgain\nL2164@P16: Angelos Chaniotis\nL2165@P16: Nicola Di Cosmo\nL2166@P16: Didier Fassin\nL2167@P16: Patrick J. Geary\nL2168@P16: Peter Goddard\nL2169@P16: Helmut Hofer\nL2170@P16: Piet Hut\nL2171@P16: Jonathan Israel\nL2172@P16: Stanislas Leibler\nL2173@P16: Robert MacPherson\nL2174@P16: Juan 